{
  "title": "What time is it?",
  "title_target": "Cén t-am é?",
  "goal": "Tell the time, name days and months, and make an appointment.",
  "bekal": [
    "tell the time with *a chlog*, *tar éis*, and *chun*",
    "use *ar a …* to say at what time something happens",
    "name days with *Dé …* and months with *i mí …*"
  ],
  "dialogs": [
    {
      "title": "Comhrá — Coinne a dhéanamh",
      "intro": "In a college corridor. Niamh is in a hurry, and Budi wants to invite her to a match.",
      "lines": [
        {
          "speaker": "Niamh:",
          "text": "Cén t-am é anois?",
          "id": "What time is it?",
          "literal": "what time it now?"
        },
        {
          "speaker": "Budi:",
          "text": "Tá sé ceathrú chun a dó.",
          "id": "It's a quarter to two.",
          "literal": "is it quarter to the two."
        },
        {
          "speaker": "Niamh:",
          "text": "Ó, tá mé déanach! Tosaíonn an rang ar a dó.",
          "id": "Oh, I'm late! The class starts at two.",
          "literal": "oh, am I late! starts the class on the two."
        },
        {
          "speaker": "Budi:",
          "text": "Cén lá atá an rang?",
          "id": "What days is the class?",
          "literal": "what day that-is the class?"
        },
        {
          "speaker": "Niamh:",
          "text": "Dé Luain agus Déardaoin, ar a dó. Agus Dé hAoine ar a deich ar maidin.",
          "id": "Monday and Thursday at two. And Friday at ten in the morning.",
          "literal": "day Monday and Thursday, on the two. and day Friday on the ten in the morning."
        },
        {
          "speaker": "Budi:",
          "text": "An bhfuil tú saor Dé Sathairn?",
          "id": "Are you free on Saturday?",
          "literal": "question are you free day Saturday?"
        },
        {
          "speaker": "Niamh:",
          "text": "Tá. Cén fáth?",
          "id": "Yes. Why?",
          "literal": "am. what reason?"
        },
        {
          "speaker": "Budi:",
          "text": "Tá cluiche ann ar a trí. Ar mhaith leat teacht?",
          "id": "There's a match at three. Would you like to come?",
          "literal": "is match in-it on the three. question-would good with-you come?"
        },
        {
          "speaker": "Niamh:",
          "text": "Ba mhaith! Cén t-am?",
          "id": "I'd love to! What time?",
          "literal": "would good! what time?"
        },
        {
          "speaker": "Budi:",
          "text": "Ar leathuair tar éis a dó, ag an stáisiún.",
          "id": "At half past two, at the station.",
          "literal": "on half-hour after the two, at the station."
        },
        {
          "speaker": "Niamh:",
          "text": "Go breá. Feicfidh mé thú Dé Sathairn.",
          "id": "Great. See you on Saturday.",
          "literal": "fine. will-see I you day Saturday."
        },
        {
          "speaker": "Budi:",
          "text": "Slán go fóill!",
          "id": "See you soon!",
          "literal": "safe till a-while!"
        }
      ],
      "notes": [
        "**Clock times use the counting numbers, *a* and all.** *Tá sé a dó a chlog* — \"it's two o'clock\". The word *a chlog* (from English *o'clock*, itself from *of the clock*) is softened by the little word *a* before it — not by the number. The numbers themselves soften nothing here: compare *deich nóiméad* and *cúig euro*, which stay intact. For times that aren't on the hour, *a chlog* is dropped: *ceathrú chun a dó*, not *ceathrú chun a dó a chlog*.",
        "**Tar éis for past, chun for to.** *Deich tar éis a trí* = \"ten after three\", i.e. 3:10. *Deich chun a trí* = \"ten to three\", i.e. 2:50. Like English, Irish counts back from the next hour once you pass the half hour.",
        "**Ar a dó — at two.** To say **when** something happens, put *ar* before the number: *ar a hocht* (at eight), *ar leathuair tar éis a naoi* (at half past nine). It's the same *ar* as in *Tá ocras orm*; Irish puts the time \"on\" the event.",
        "**Dé Luain, Déardaoin — the word Dé already means \"on the day of\".** So *Dé Luain* already means \"on Monday\", with no extra preposition needed. To name the day itself as a noun — \"Monday\" as a thing — use *an Luan*. Note *Déardaoin* (Thursday), fused into one word, and *Dé hAoine* (Friday), which gets an *h* because *Aoine* starts with a vowel."
      ]
    }
  ],
